# Reanimated / Gesture Handler on Android

Use these patterns in components that use `useAnimatedStyle` with `GestureDetector` and `runOnJS`, so taps don't crash on Android.

## 1. No JS props inside useAnimatedStyle

Worklets run on the UI thread. Reading changing JS props (e.g. `selected`) inside `useAnimatedStyle` can crash on Android.

- Drive all animated style values from **shared values** only.
- For colors that depend on selection: update a shared value (e.g. `borderOpacity`) in `useEffect` when the prop changes, then in the worklet use `interpolateColor(sharedValue.value, [0, 1], [colorA, colorB])` instead of `selected ? colorA : colorB`.

```tsx
// BAD – reading `selected` in worklet
const animatedStyle = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
  backgroundColor: selected ? colors.accentSoft : colors.backgroundCard,
}));

// GOOD – only shared values in worklet
useEffect(() => {
  borderOpacity.value = withTiming(selected ? 1 : 0, { duration: 200 });
}, [selected]);
const animatedStyle = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
  backgroundColor: interpolateColor(
    borderOpacity.value,
    [0, 1],
    [colors.backgroundCard, colors.accentSoft]
  ),
}));
```

## 2. Stable callback for runOnJS

`runOnJS(handleTap)()` must invoke a stable function. If the parent passes an inline `onSelect`, it changes every render and can cause stale closures / crashes on Android.

- Keep the latest callback in a ref and call that ref from the gesture handler so the function passed to `runOnJS` never changes.

```tsx
const onSelectRef = useRef(onSelect);
onSelectRef.current = onSelect;

const handleTap = useCallback(() => {
  if (Platform.OS === 'ios') Haptics.impactAsync(Haptics.ImpactFeedbackStyle.Light);
  onSelectRef.current();
}, []);

const tap = Gesture.Tap()
  .onFinalize((_, success) => {
    if (success) runOnJS(handleTap)();
  });
```

Reference: fixes applied in `SelectCard.tsx` and `MultiSelectPill.tsx`.
